            "content": [
              {
                "type": "output_text",
                "text": "The latest major news on NASA’s **Mars Perseverance rover** is that it has been studying extremely ancient rocks on the rim of Jezero Crater and has also reached a big driving milestone.\n\n- **Ancient impact record found:** NASA reports that Perseverance has examined a 245-foot-thick / 75-meter-thick stack of layered rock called the **“Broom Point member”** on Jezero Crater’s rim. Scientists think these rocks are **more than 3.9 billion years old** and were built up by repeated asteroid impacts, preserving a rare record from early solar-system history【5:2†source】.  \n- **Why it matters:** Because Mars lacks Earth-style plate tectonics, very old crustal records can remain preserved. That means Perseverance may be giving scientists a view into a period of planetary history that Earth has largely erased【5:3†source】.  \n- **New views from the rover:** NASA also released a sweeping Mastcam-Z view looking back toward the outside lip of Jezero Crater from an area called **Broom Point**【5:1†source】.  \n- **Marathon milestone:** Perseverance recently completed the equivalent of a full marathon on Mars — **26.2 miles / 42.195 km** — reaching that distance on its **1,890th Martian day**, much faster than Opportunity did【5:0†source】. NASA’s Mars Reconnaissance Orbiter/HiRISE captured the rover and its tracks from orbit around the time of the milestone【5:2†source】.\n\nIn short: Perseverance is now exploring some of the oldest terrain it has ever studied, while continuing to prove its durability by covering marathon-level distance across Mars.",
